WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.275
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:09.500
EVREN ERYUREK: Good afternoon.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.094
How's everyone doing?

00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:11.837
AUDIENCE: Great.

00:00:11.837 --> 00:00:12.670
EVREN ERYUREK: Nice.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.740
It's 2 o'clock,
we're ready, right?

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:16.970
My name is Evren.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.540
I'm the director of product
management and data analytics

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.610
organization in Google Cloud.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.520
And today I have
my guest, Michelle.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.710
She'll join us in a few seconds.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.290
If you want, you can
come up here too.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:33.490
And today's topic is data
governance in this world.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.600
And we've got a lot of
questions yesterday.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.620
Even during my presentation for
streaming analytics, one of you

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:39.660
have asked--

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.130
I think you're there, thank you.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.270
I love that topic.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:46.000
This is something we're
going to talk about probably

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.520
at length over the years.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.590
But I wanted to start what
we're going to discuss today.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.450
And Michelle is going to cover
a lot of insight for you guys.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:58.660
I'm really excited to
have her here with us.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.390
And we're going to talk about
what data governance is,

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:02.740
what companies are doing.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:03.500
In the end.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.260
I'm going to a little
cover about what

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.680
we're doing on Google Cloud
to help you really deal

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:13.780
with your world and how these
things can be actually enabling

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.400
capabilities to grow
your businesses.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.160
So let's talk about data.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.760
Data is getting
bigger and bigger.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.510
I think I mentioned
this yesterday, too.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:27.490
By 2020, every one of us
in the world, every second

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:31.930
will produce about two
megabits of data every second.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.580
That's a lot of data.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.375
Sum them up-- there is
about 8 billion of us.

00:01:36.375 --> 00:01:37.500
That's quite a bit of them.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.000
MICHELE GOETZ:
That's a lot of data.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.250
EVREN ERYUREK: Look at all those
things that we're seeing today.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.610
There are businesses
being built around us,

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.050
but still we're
scratching the surface

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.360
of what we can do with data.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.660
Either we are not ready,
either we're afraid of it,

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.420
or we don't have the
right tools for it.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.840
Who remembers the
Vs of the big data?

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.800
Remember, velocity,
variety, volume.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.590
Those three things are still
kicking really, really high.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:14.010
Take a look at the volume
and variety, it's tripling.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:18.090
Fantastic for us because
you can take this data

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:24.440
and generate some great
insights for your businesses.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.680
But if you're not
prepared to deal with it,

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.940
that won't help you.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.330
Look at the velocity, it
is shaping up immensely.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.710
Yesterday, we talked
about streaming.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:35.830
And I had a partner
on stage with me,

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:39.580
AB tested, they were talking
about how they were utilizing

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:44.990
this real-time data to generate
revenues for their customers.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.500
You can do the same
if you can harness

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.710
how you can use the data,
if you can turn governance

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.280
into revenue generating
mechanism for yourself.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.270
We are seeing
businesses building data

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:01.960
and event-driven
structures for themselves.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.250
You can do the same.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.740
We have the platform for you,
we have the means for you.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.240
There are a lot of things
that we're learning.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.470
But I said, we're not
really using all the data

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:12.910
available to enhance.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:16.990
Take a look at this, only
1% of the unstructured data

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.010
is in use.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.540
Only 1%.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:23.410
If you look at the 50%
of the structured data

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:28.060
is still sitting and not being
used for decision-making.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:31.420
We're leaving it, we're
leaving value behind us.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.950
What will it take for
us to really get there?

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:38.803
I think all of us know why
we can't do this, right?

00:03:38.803 --> 00:03:39.345
It's complex.

00:03:39.345 --> 00:03:39.950
MICHELE GOETZ: It's super hard.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.970
EVREN ERYUREK: Data is
distributed everywhere,

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.940
loss of legacy
applications, silos,

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.730
we may not have the talent.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.520
But there is still a way.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.465
What do you think?

00:03:50.465 --> 00:03:53.265
MICHELE GOETZ: Well, we
have to address people.

00:03:53.265 --> 00:03:53.550
EVREN ERYUREK:
What do you think?

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:54.467
MICHELE GOETZ: I know.

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.040
We have to address people.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:57.580
What do we really want?

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.490
We want to be able
to use it, we want

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.290
to be able to
interact with it, we

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:03.780
want to be able to
play with our data.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.920
But our experience around
our data is really--

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.180
we struggle with it.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.090
We struggle with it
on a small data scale.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.170
And if we struggle
with it there,

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.360
we're going to struggle
with it on a big data scale.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.382
Who owns the data?

00:04:19.382 --> 00:04:21.100
EVREN ERYUREK: All
right, some guesses.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.570
It has to be interactive.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:23.380
Who owns the data?

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:24.755
MICHELE GOETZ:
Who owns the data?

00:04:24.755 --> 00:04:26.814
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
generated it.

00:04:26.814 --> 00:04:28.930
EVREN ERYUREK: Good, all
right, that's one way.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:29.862
AUDIENCE: Collectors.

00:04:29.862 --> 00:04:34.040
EVREN ERYUREK: Collectors, OK.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.114
Do you own your data?

00:04:36.114 --> 00:04:37.108
MICHELE GOETZ: No.

00:04:37.108 --> 00:04:38.788
AUDIENCE: The company
owns the data.

00:04:38.788 --> 00:04:40.330
EVREN ERYUREK:
Company owns the data.

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.122
MICHELE GOETZ: These
are way better answers

00:04:42.122 --> 00:04:43.270
than what I usually get.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.796
EVREN ERYUREK: That's good, see.

00:04:45.796 --> 00:04:48.280
MICHELE GOETZ: We have
smart Googlers in the room.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.570
A few years ago
we started asking

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.610
the question, who
owns the data, looking

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:59.780
at it through the lens of saying
where's the data strategy sit,

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.000
where does governance
sit, where does master

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.080
data management sit-- all things
that help us better understand

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.740
and take advantage of our
information to make decisions,

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:13.610
to grow our revenues, to
great customer experiences.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.760
And data decision-makers,
what do they say?

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.420
IT owns it, 50% of them.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:20.740
IT, take care of it.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:22.680
EVREN ERYUREK: Who's
in charge here?

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.050
Do you own the data?

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:24.808
AUDIENCE: No.

00:05:24.808 --> 00:05:26.600
MICHELE GOETZ: Do you
want to own the data?

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:27.600
AUDIENCE: No.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.588
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:29.588 --> 00:05:30.872
MICHELE GOETZ: No.

00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:32.900
EVREN ERYUREK: Great, great.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.320
MICHELE GOETZ: So what do we do?

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.650
So that was back in 2015.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:39.380
In 2018, we asked
the question again.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.410
And what did the data
decision-makers say?

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:45.350
They said, IT still owns
it, and more of them

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:46.730
said that IT owns it.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.710
So what does that actually mean?

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:52.870
You said who created it,
where does it come from,

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.170
who's taking care of it.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.990
And it really
boils down to this.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.250
I want my data to work.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:05.290
Stop frustrating me every
time I go into my application,

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:07.630
every time I go into my
business intelligence,

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.580
every time I'm trying
to run analytics,

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.247
every time I'm just
trying to figure out

00:06:11.247 --> 00:06:14.760
who my customer is so I
can have a grand old party.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.270
EVREN ERYUREK: Don't
lock your data.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:17.120
MICHELE GOETZ: Don't lock it up.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:17.680
EVREN ERYUREK: Make
it work for you.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.430
MICHELE GOETZ: Make
it work for you.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.940
And when they talk about
or when our respondents

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:25.030
talked about IT
owns the data, it's

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.435
because the complexity
of it is a nightmare.

00:06:27.435 --> 00:06:28.810
It's what we deal
with every day.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:31.270
We need to simplify it.

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:35.920
So what we're going to do is now
take this to data governance,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.310
because data governance
really is that accelerator

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:40.400
to make it happen.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.966
Do you believe that?

00:06:41.966 --> 00:06:43.760
EVREN ERYUREK: And
she will convince

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.842
you guys how data
governance is an accelerator

00:06:46.842 --> 00:06:47.550
for the business.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.340
MICHELE GOETZ: Accelerator.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:51.400
So let's introduce you
to data governance first.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.525
EVREN ERYUREK: There we go.

00:06:52.525 --> 00:06:54.900
So if you look at data
governance, little history.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:57.550
If you go back, some
of you are coming

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:01.540
from the very highly regulated
industries back in 2007.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:05.490
It was specific to the
departments, very specific.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.040
In 2008, if you
remember, there was

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:12.180
this crisis, lots of regulation
around the financial data.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:15.270
What can be done, who can
be done, very prescriptive.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:16.770
Remember those days?

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:17.970
Things have changed.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.370
But if you look at
today where we are,

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.350
GDPR happened in Europe.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:25.760
In here, CCPA is happening.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.290
Don't be afraid of it.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:32.450
It's actually going to help you
make more sense from your data.

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:36.080
What's driving the
governance in the data

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.320
governance in the initiatives?

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:43.430
MICHELE GOETZ: We want to
connect to our customers,

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:45.770
see who they are,
get a customer 360.

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:48.980
We want to control our
data, protect ourselves,

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:50.550
stay in compliance.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.570
We know that our
regulators look at how

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:57.290
we use data and the type of
data that we're collecting

00:07:57.290 --> 00:07:58.940
to say how we're doing.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:00.875
We just don't feel
like we're in control.

00:08:00.875 --> 00:08:02.375
It's that make that
data work thing.

00:08:02.375 --> 00:08:02.660
EVREN ERYUREK: Exactly.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:04.743
MICHELE GOETZ: If we don't
have great experiences,

00:08:04.743 --> 00:08:07.280
how can we trust our
information to make decisions.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:09.920
EVREN ERYUREK: Every
one of them are actually

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.810
asking about these things.

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:15.140
The complexity of the
data, lack of control--

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.690
that's old, maybe the
perception of control--

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:20.990
and how we can actually
operate and drive efficiencies

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:23.000
around it and risk management.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.790
These are all items that are
driving the governance needs.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.270
Now it is about policy,
it's about people,

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.130
it's about technology.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:35.030
Now you might say, hey, I heard
the same thing for other things

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:36.659
too, but it's absolutely true.

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:39.380
But there are specific
cases that we can actually

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.049
leverage and build
together to really make

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:43.309
this thing work for you.

00:08:43.309 --> 00:08:45.555
It is going to be
driving the success.

00:08:45.555 --> 00:08:46.430
MICHELE GOETZ: Right.

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:48.320
EVREN ERYUREK: How
does it come to rescue?

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.711
MICHELE GOETZ: How does
it come to the rescue?

00:08:50.711 --> 00:08:53.360
Well, data governance is
like any other competency

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.370
in our practice in our business.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:57.080
People, process, technology.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.780
Sounds really boring
on the surface of it,

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:03.150
but it's that brain trust
that comes together.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:07.730
Where not only are you trying
to drive down the complexity,

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:09.600
whether you're trying
to mitigate risk,

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.540
but on the other hand
we do have strategies

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:14.310
of getting value from our data.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:18.080
And we need policies that
enable us to democratize it

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.420
and to find those
great insights.

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.590
And so when we talk
about data governance

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:25.440
as that starting
point for that value

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.490
in accelerating our
business forward,

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:29.990
that's how we should really be
thinking about our governance

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:32.120
program, as a
strategic competency.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:33.080
It's not just--

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:35.377
EVREN ERYUREK: Have you
seen examples of how

00:09:35.377 --> 00:09:36.460
people operationalized it?

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:37.400
MICHELE GOETZ: Oh, yeah.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:38.020
EVREN ERYUREK: Is it lowball?

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:39.230
MICHELE GOETZ: Yeah,
it's a lowball.

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:39.910
Thank you.

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:41.690
EVREN ERYUREK: You're welcome.

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:43.190
Who does this thing
well these days?

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:46.670
MICHELE GOETZ: So I
think John Deere has done

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.275
some really interesting things.

00:09:48.275 --> 00:09:50.600
EVREN ERYUREK: Anyone in
here in the world of where

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.800
John Deere is similar to?

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.500
Some manufacturing, operations?

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:56.716
MICHELE GOETZ: Farming.

00:09:56.716 --> 00:09:58.799
EVREN ERYUREK: Well, maybe
they'll take advantage.

00:09:58.799 --> 00:09:59.940
MICHELE GOETZ: All right.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.580
Let's tell a little bit
of story about this.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.190
Just like every
other company here,

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.700
all of you here
governance is a nightmare.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.670
And when we've
started on big data

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.370
and predictive and
prescriptive analytics,

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:16.510
we don't want anything
getting in our way.

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:17.860
So what do we do?

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:21.000
We grab the data we need,
and we start analyzing

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.590
it and we build insights.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:24.720
This is what John Deere
was really focused on.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.040
How do we help farming
communities drive better crop

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:29.800
yields?

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:34.110
How do we better manage the
finances around crop insurance,

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:35.860
for example.

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:39.390
And so they were just grabbing
all of this IoT data off

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:43.560
of their tractors, watching how
those tractors were operating

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:49.090
in the fields, how the seeds
and the fertilizer was going,

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:53.070
how the harvesting was
happening, relating this

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:56.850
all back to their customers and
the finances to support this,

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.800
as well as the
dealerships who are

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:04.470
helping with a lot of the
fixes on the equipment.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:05.910
What happened, though?

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:07.105
They ran into a problem.

00:11:07.105 --> 00:11:08.730
They weren't thinking
about governance,

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:11.700
they weren't thinking
about what is the org

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:15.630
structure of themselves
in order to relate back

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.550
to their customers
and dealerships.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.410
They didn't have
the proper master

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:22.230
data around their customers
and their dealerships.

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.910
So you can think
about how do you

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.370
create all these
relationships, and really

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:29.700
understand what
information can I share

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:31.505
or should I share with whom.

00:11:31.505 --> 00:11:32.880
There are things
that agronomists

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.995
don't want to share
with the dealerships,

00:11:34.995 --> 00:11:36.870
or they don't want to
share with their banks.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:38.650
It's very personal.

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:42.270
And when you move
equipment around

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:45.970
in big farming
areas, what happens?

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:47.797
They don't just tractor
down the street.

00:11:47.797 --> 00:11:49.130
EVREN ERYUREK: It's a nightmare.

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:51.755
MICHELE GOETZ: You're not always
stuck behind the International

00:11:51.755 --> 00:11:53.280
Harvester.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.560
Sometimes they're put on
flat beds and they move.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.680
What happens when you
move that tractor 50 miles

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:02.062
and you're trying to figure
out how your harvest is going?

00:12:02.062 --> 00:12:02.770
That's a problem.

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:06.280
So a lot of data quality issues
were starting to come up.

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:09.523
So what they really
did is they took a look

00:12:09.523 --> 00:12:13.270
at how they were moving
forward very quickly.

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:17.440
They were running a DevOps
agile development practice,

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:20.590
not thinking about
governance as requirements.

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:23.860
Now they start to bring
those business requirements

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.920
in that really drive the
policies, the understanding

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:29.620
about who their customers are,
who their dealerships are,

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:31.750
who they are as an
org, understanding

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:33.580
how their equipment runs.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:38.080
And it's not just this control
thing that drives you away,

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:40.910
but it actually brings
the value back in.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:42.970
And so rather than
struggling for another year

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:45.400
with these practices,
they're able to really drive

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.770
forward very, very quickly
because the information is

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:50.370
trusted and the value of the
insights really comes together.

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:50.780
EVREN ERYUREK: Absolutely.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:53.830
This is why we wanted to share
some of these examples with you

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:57.940
early on because if you're on
this path and start actually

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.490
looking at it, down the road
you'll start seeing, hey,

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.360
we didn't really
think about these.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:06.130
It's best to really consider
what we need to do collectively

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:08.620
as you're building your
operations around data,

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:11.320
around events and what you
want to do around big data,

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:13.600
because governance is
going to be your guide.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:15.710
Your governance is
going to be your help.

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:17.477
It's going to be
there to really help

00:13:17.477 --> 00:13:19.060
you accelerate some
of the things that

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:21.130
are coming in your
way that you may not

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:23.136
be considering it today.

00:13:23.136 --> 00:13:25.570
MICHELE GOETZ: So how do
you start to look at this?

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:28.090
Well, you want to have
a vision for the future.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:29.132
You want a success story.

00:13:29.132 --> 00:13:30.215
EVREN ERYUREK: Absolutely.

00:13:30.215 --> 00:13:33.050
MICHELE GOETZ: You want to
know what success looks like.

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:36.430
So let's connect the dots
between what information

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:37.015
that we have.

00:13:37.015 --> 00:13:38.990
EVREN ERYUREK: Does it
matter which industry,

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:39.835
the other industry?

00:13:39.835 --> 00:13:41.542
MICHELE GOETZ: No difference.

00:13:41.542 --> 00:13:42.750
EVREN ERYUREK: No difference.

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:43.060
MICHELE GOETZ: No difference.

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:44.510
EVREN ERYUREK: So they may
not be the farmers in here.

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:45.665
MICHELE GOETZ: You don't
have to be a farmer.

00:13:45.665 --> 00:13:46.665
EVREN ERYUREK: OK, good.

00:13:46.665 --> 00:13:48.970
MICHELE GOETZ: You don't
have to be a mechanic.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:51.010
You don't have to
be an engineer.

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:52.500
You can be all of those things.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:53.730
EVREN ERYUREK: But they have
supply chains, some of them.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.225
MICHELE GOETZ: They
have supply chains.

00:13:55.225 --> 00:13:57.040
EVREN ERYUREK: They have
systems that they run?

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:57.920
MICHELE GOETZ: They do.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.200
They've got financial systems.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:00.520
It's your business.

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.470
It doesn't matter.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:05.170
And everybody has a vision for
where their business should go

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:06.880
and what those objectives are.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.640
So build your success
story around that.

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:13.400
Start with where are my
business opportunities.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:15.870
How is data going to
shape that for me?

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:17.955
Where do I want to take
my business forward?

00:14:17.955 --> 00:14:19.080
EVREN ERYUREK: That's true.

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:20.830
MICHELE GOETZ: John
Deere's example

00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:25.320
there is to push the envelope
on not just being a farming

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:26.640
equipment company.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:30.810
They want to help you do a
better job at bringing products

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:32.970
to market yourself.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:35.670
And recognizing-- being
real about the challenges

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:36.400
that you face.

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:38.970
Not just that you're missing
information, but really

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.520
holistically looking at
how you interact and engage

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.890
with that data and how you're
going to put it to use.

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.350
Look at the people and
processes, the experiences

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:48.860
around that data from
the point of bringing it

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:50.940
in to the point of where
it's being presented

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:52.690
to the point where
you're making decisions

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:54.330
and taking actions off of that.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:57.690
Now you can start looking at
a much more holistic solution

00:14:57.690 --> 00:14:59.700
for your data
strategies overall,

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:02.730
for the management of that,
and taking into account

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:06.270
the governance policies that
are going to happen from it.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:10.620
And then it boils down to, how
do I know I did a great job?

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:11.580
How do I sell myself?

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:12.930
How do I tell that story?

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:14.880
How do I get that
next promotion?

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:18.760
It's what I do great from a
data management perspective.

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:21.810
What did I do great from a
data governance perspective.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:25.750
And what did I do great
from a business perspective.

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:28.440
So you have three
different points of view.

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:33.780
For every stakeholder, that
is part of delivering value.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:37.980
And 69% of companies
who do this,

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:44.100
who think from a
business-led perspective,

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.260
have data governance
programs in place.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:48.720
So that doesn't look
like putting the brakes

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:49.920
on your business, does it?

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:51.842
EVREN ERYUREK: No,
they have grown.

00:15:51.842 --> 00:15:53.300
MICHELE GOETZ: And
the way that you

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:55.970
start to enable and
drive culture change

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:59.240
around that is, how do
you know the data is good?

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:03.920
Where do you put in these
cues as you bring data in,

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:05.150
as you're going to use it?

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:08.090
As you distribute that
out to your customers,

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:10.550
you are looking at social
mechanisms that say,

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:12.020
I like my data.

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.870
And you see the thumbs up,
you see a star that says,

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:17.690
it's certified and it's
managed and it's governed well.

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:20.450
So I know somebody is
taking a look at it

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:26.240
and has a vested interest in my
personal success with the data.

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:30.020
And there's also cues from a
governance perspective to say,

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.570
are we hitting up
against any regulations?

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:34.620
Are there risks from our data?

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:38.060
So really thinking holistically
around those success factors

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:40.670
and metrics and KPIs,
and bringing that back

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:43.520
into the experience to
drive an appropriate culture

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:44.360
around that.

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:47.330
EVREN ERYUREK: No more reactive,
much more proactive approach.

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:49.280
MICHELE GOETZ: Exactly.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:49.940
It's social.

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:50.857
EVREN ERYUREK: I know.

00:16:50.857 --> 00:16:52.416
It is all social.

00:16:52.416 --> 00:16:54.830
MICHELE GOETZ: Roles
and responsibilities.

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:56.360
Oftentimes we
think of governance

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:59.270
as the big bureaucracy
at the top.

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:01.790
And we have our
executive councils

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:04.290
and there's a lot
of conversation.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:06.210
And then you say, what happens?

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:08.130
Well, now I'm going
to go fix the data.

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:11.300
But really what we're
doing is driving a hub

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:14.450
and spoke culture around this.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.750
Having centers of gravity
where expertise about the data

00:17:17.750 --> 00:17:20.690
and the value from the
data is going to come in.

00:17:20.690 --> 00:17:24.170
And even looking at
that directly at the end

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:27.119
points of your organization.

00:17:27.119 --> 00:17:29.300
When you said who's
creating the data,

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:33.560
they're stewards and custodians,
not just creationists.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:35.907
They have a vested
responsibility

00:17:35.907 --> 00:17:38.240
in ensuring that the information
that they're collecting

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:40.770
and bringing in not
only works for them,

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:43.550
but will also help drive things
and continue to pay it forward

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:45.690
to the rest of the organization.

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:48.440
So when we talk about the
experience of data and making

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.720
data work, don't
forget your end users.

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:53.330
Don't forget the
fact that nobody

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:56.720
likes to jump through 17
different applications

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:59.270
to figure out how to provide
customer support when somebody

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:01.250
has got a problem with
their insurance policy

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:03.440
and needs questions answered.

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:06.980
And, oh by the way, I've been
through that process, which

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:09.560
is why I explain it that way.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:11.630
And when you're a
data scientist and you

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:16.040
want to go in and grab data and
manipulate it and prepare it,

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:18.590
that's great
information and insight

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:21.230
for the data teams
coming back where

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:24.200
they have to now interpret
and deploy those models

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:26.690
and rationalize them
against policies.

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:30.110
So building a mechanism
for tribal knowledge

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:32.180
to come through is
what we talk about when

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:35.150
we say a change in culture
and bringing data governance

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:37.530
to the edge of your business.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:39.410
Now what happens?

00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:42.980
You need an awesome
delivery team to do that.

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:46.730
Who's going to instrument
across infrastructure, your data

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:50.600
sources, and your end
points, from applications

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:53.810
to devices to automation?

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:57.260
We need engineering
and DataOps right

00:18:57.260 --> 00:18:59.360
within our chief data office.

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:01.640
They are the
masters of delivery,

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:03.620
building the services
and the pipelines that

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:04.850
are going to deliver--

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:06.800
taking, just like
John Deere did,

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:08.810
those data governance
requirements and data

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:11.150
requirements, mashing
them together,

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:13.970
and really thinking succinctly
about where that drives

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:16.238
into the solutions that happen.

00:19:16.238 --> 00:19:17.780
EVREN ERYUREK: The
chief data officer

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:19.770
that you saw is a trend.

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:23.480
If you look at the next
18 months, last 18 months,

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:24.920
there's a huge tick.

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:27.170
And you're going to
actually see that continuing

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:29.840
because everybody is trying
to figure out what their data

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.340
strategy is, and they're trying
to build their organization

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:32.840
around it.

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:33.170
MICHELE GOETZ: Exactly.

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:35.337
EVREN ERYUREK: And each of
those chief data officers

00:19:35.337 --> 00:19:37.550
are coming with
the mindset of, OK,

00:19:37.550 --> 00:19:40.200
what do I do to get more
value out of my data?

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:43.460
That's exactly the organization
they're trying to establish.

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:45.380
I am willing to bet
some of you are already

00:19:45.380 --> 00:19:46.820
in those organizations today.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:48.290
MICHELE GOETZ: Exactly.

00:19:48.290 --> 00:19:50.900
And you need that
strategic leader

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:52.610
because the complexity
around this,

00:19:52.610 --> 00:19:54.410
not only in terms
of what you want

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:58.130
to aspire to with your data,
but how you operationalize that,

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:02.270
not just from team and delivery,
but holistically your program

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:03.140
has to come out.

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.690
Look at all these
different processes that

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:08.000
are driving towards
outcomes, managing

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:11.570
around both offensive and
defensive responsibilities,

00:20:11.570 --> 00:20:14.460
taking into account
today, more and more,

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.600
especially as GDPR
and CCPA have on

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:21.590
and security breaches
are at record levels,

00:20:21.590 --> 00:20:23.690
security is brought
into the fold.

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:26.360
It doesn't just sit
over here anymore.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:29.870
When you're moving into
more complex data science

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:32.910
and artificial
intelligence capabilities,

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:36.260
you are now tapping
into historical archives

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:39.980
of your data, turning cold
storage into hot storage.

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.587
What is the life cycle of that?

00:20:42.587 --> 00:20:43.420
How do manage there?

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:44.630
EVREN ERYUREK: Could
you have used that data

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:45.530
that was sitting there?

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:45.800
MICHELE GOETZ: Right.

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:48.320
EVREN ERYUREK: Do you actually
know the lineage of your data?

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.237
Do you know if you can
actually use that data?

00:20:50.237 --> 00:20:52.605
Do you have the process
to identify all those?

00:20:52.605 --> 00:20:54.000
MICHELE GOETZ: Exactly.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:57.570
And so creating those policies
of recognizing what they are,

00:20:57.570 --> 00:21:00.690
communicating them,
training the organization

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:05.100
to do that, all these different
policies are resolving

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:07.710
themselves around those
outcomes and objectives

00:21:07.710 --> 00:21:10.200
that you're looking at,
both from a data governance

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:12.390
point-of-view, as well as
your business policies,

00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:14.380
as well as regulatory
and risk management.

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:17.130
EVREN ERYUREK: Did
you see some instances

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:21.440
where some good dynamic policy
investments and companies are

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:25.850
really actually integrating
into their business practices?

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:27.685
MICHELE GOETZ: So.

00:21:27.685 --> 00:21:30.060
EVREN ERYUREK: Have you, maybe.

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:31.830
MICHELE GOETZ: I
think the oil and gas

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:36.990
industry is amazing in the way
that they think about this.

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:44.740
It's very complicated on
drawing oil out of the ground.

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:48.240
So if you think about the
complexity of their partner

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:54.000
ecosystem to just build the
drill to put down the well,

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:57.030
and then run all of the
management and maintenance off

00:21:57.030 --> 00:22:01.620
of that, handling
inventory and parts,

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:05.850
they really operationalize
the data to the tasks.

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:08.940
And so those policies have
to balance against things

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:11.940
like trusted networks
to share information,

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:15.150
ensuring that parts aren't
coming from the black market

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:17.400
and really are to spec and
who they've purchased from

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.700
so they don't have a drill
go down and lose $1 million

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:21.960
an hour.

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:26.430
So you can really see where
policies, business policies,

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:31.770
risk policies are all brought
together just to bring oil

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:33.120
to market.

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.163
Or energy, I mean,
energy is the same thing.

00:22:35.163 --> 00:22:36.705
If you're on electric
cars, the same.

00:22:36.705 --> 00:22:37.540
EVREN ERYUREK: [INAUDIBLE]
about the distribution.

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:38.970
MICHELE GOETZ: Exactly.

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:44.100
So policies are really coming
together in that manner, too.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:45.960
How do you deal
with the complexity?

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:49.990
Oh my goodness, tech has
go to take care of it.

00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:52.278
EVREN ERYUREK: Automation?

00:22:52.278 --> 00:22:54.070
Somehow we've got to
automate these things?

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:56.130
MICHELE GOETZ:
Somehow, some way.

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:00.830
Without giving you
too much credence,

00:23:00.830 --> 00:23:05.640
you need a Google in the
center of your environment.

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:07.430
And why do I say that?

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:11.660
We think of data and
how you manage it

00:23:11.660 --> 00:23:14.180
around this notion of metadata.

00:23:14.180 --> 00:23:16.250
How do we describe
our information,

00:23:16.250 --> 00:23:18.920
how do we set policies
around that information,

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:21.450
how do we see ownership
for that data,

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:23.995
and where is it located
what's the lineage of it.

00:23:23.995 --> 00:23:26.412
EVREN ERYUREK: It's [? double ?]
vary your data assets are

00:23:26.412 --> 00:23:27.505
to begin with, right?

00:23:27.505 --> 00:23:28.880
MICHELE GOETZ:
The first thing we

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:30.830
do is profile our
information and capture

00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:33.290
all of that metadata.

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:36.710
What does that metadata
mean for us though

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:38.747
is the bigger question.

00:23:38.747 --> 00:23:40.580
And so then our governance
processes come in

00:23:40.580 --> 00:23:41.330
and what do we do?

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:43.550
We slap a glossary on top of it.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:45.800
So that we put it
into business context.

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:47.842
Now we've got an awesome
data governance program

00:23:47.842 --> 00:23:49.550
where data governance
and data management

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:50.570
are all coming together.

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:52.940
And we'll figure out how to
connect all of our systems

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:55.430
and make our services
and pipelines flow.

00:23:55.430 --> 00:23:57.980
And we should be off and
running to the races,

00:23:57.980 --> 00:23:59.540
and we're all set.

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:02.870
But in the world where we
have to change the experience,

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:06.740
where democratizing data
is much more important

00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:10.910
than it was in the past,
where data engineers don't

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:13.820
stop at bringing
data into a data lake

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:16.138
or into another
type of repository,

00:24:16.138 --> 00:24:17.930
but they have to think
about that delivery,

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:20.900
they have to think about
presentation of information,

00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:22.710
you need a marketplace.

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:24.680
What does that
marketplace look like?

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:28.310
So catalogs today
start to become

00:24:28.310 --> 00:24:32.390
that center of the universe,
of not just describing

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:35.810
the physical, logical, and
semantic aspects of the data,

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:38.210
not just housing all
of those policies

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:42.470
and ownerships and practices,
but also becoming that point,

00:24:42.470 --> 00:24:47.390
both from services, as well as
direct access to information.

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:49.980
So that you have--
and I hate to say it,

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:53.900
we're back to a
knowledge center, guys.

00:24:53.900 --> 00:24:55.367
Don't twitch if
you remember Lotus.

00:24:55.367 --> 00:24:57.200
EVREN ERYUREK: That's
part of the knowledge.

00:24:57.200 --> 00:24:58.940
MICHELE GOETZ: It's so much
smarter because machine

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:00.110
learning is at the heart of it.

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:00.490
EVREN ERYUREK: Exactly.

00:25:00.490 --> 00:25:01.970
MICHELE GOETZ: So
all those social

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:04.530
cues that we talked
about earlier,

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:06.650
you can see what
people are sharing.

00:25:06.650 --> 00:25:08.510
You can see what
people like, you

00:25:08.510 --> 00:25:10.580
can see what people
are changing,

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:13.590
you can see what people are
saying about information.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:15.748
And that's not just on
the consumption side--

00:25:15.748 --> 00:25:18.290
that's your governance teams,
and that's your data management

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:20.370
and engineering teams as well.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.580
So it's really important
to think about,

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:26.210
how do you bring together the
metadata facet, the business

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:28.460
glossary facet,
and the marketplace

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:31.310
and engagement,
and build services

00:25:31.310 --> 00:25:36.410
that are not just about an
ETL job or a streaming job.

00:25:36.410 --> 00:25:39.440
But look at the queries
that are running

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:40.910
against your information.

00:25:40.910 --> 00:25:43.580
Look at the Python and R
scripts that are coming out

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:45.800
of your data science notebooks.

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:49.580
All of that information really
helps you understand not only

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:51.860
what data you have and
how it can be used,

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:55.430
but where it's providing value.

00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:57.560
And this is my
English muffin slide.

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:02.600
I like talking about
the nooks and crannies

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:04.370
of where data lives.

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:07.010
Don't just think that
that metadata is only

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:08.510
in your databases.

00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:09.990
It's everywhere.

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:15.120
Let's go back to the
John Deere situation.

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:16.550
How do you manage master data?

00:26:16.550 --> 00:26:20.140
Is that in an MDM environment?

00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:22.890
It could be a Kafka topic.

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:26.460
Now you're distributing all of
these definitions and models

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:31.010
about your data across
every piece of technology.

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:33.990
Your catalogs have
to be able to profile

00:26:33.990 --> 00:26:38.370
and understand and interpret and
synchronize that information.

00:26:38.370 --> 00:26:39.900
It's really
critical-- this is how

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:42.120
you can navigate your fabrics.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:44.865
This is how you have the
appropriate oversight.

00:26:44.865 --> 00:26:47.100
EVREN ERYUREK: Don't just
think about your apps

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:48.720
and what the data
is flowing through.

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:51.150
Think about the assets,
think about new containers

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:52.290
that you're managing.

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:54.150
Think about all the
servers that your data

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:55.320
might be running through.

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:58.740
You really have to consider
all that in your system.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:01.480
MICHELE GOETZ: Get started
with it on a smaller basis.

00:27:01.480 --> 00:27:04.710
Think about your solution,
think about the pipeline first.

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:07.590
Begin there so that
you're continuously

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:11.280
accumulating this
information and starting to--

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:13.970
I don't know if there are
any gamers in the room?

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:17.530
When you've got your game map
open and it's all highlighted

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:19.650
and you see a big light
in the section of the map

00:27:19.650 --> 00:27:21.750
that you're in your
universe, and then

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:25.390
as you move around the
map starts to open up.

00:27:25.390 --> 00:27:26.700
OK, not a lot of gamers here.

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:27.560
EVREN ERYUREK: No, no so much.

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:28.435
MICHELE GOETZ: Sorry.

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:34.050
But everything
becomes illuminated

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:35.350
as you move through.

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:37.590
And that's what your
catalog should be

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:39.360
able to do and help you with.

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:42.390
It should start
illuminating iteratively

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:45.380
through your data ecosystems.

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:46.080
Lessons learned.

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.080
EVREN ERYUREK: Go ahead.

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:49.435
What did we learn?

00:27:49.435 --> 00:27:53.630
MICHELE GOETZ: One
size does not fit all.

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:57.260
Your data has to fit the
experience and the policies

00:27:57.260 --> 00:28:01.370
and procedures and culture
of your organization.

00:28:01.370 --> 00:28:03.980
It can drive massive
benefits when you tie it

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:07.850
to those business outcomes--

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:14.000
hopefully driving
income-- to deliver.

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:16.280
There is a certain
amount of centralization

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:18.110
in the organization
that is required.

00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:21.860
Somebody needs to
have and has a purview

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:23.120
over the entire landscape.

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:24.950
That's really where
that chief data officer

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:28.880
comes in and drives some
of the top-down priorities

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:31.070
and objectives for
your data, as well

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:34.100
as making sure that the
bottom-up is orchestrated

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:34.760
appropriately.

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:35.690
That's a tough job.

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:38.180
It's complex from an
operations perspective,

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:42.240
as well as from the
data perspective.

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:44.300
It requires that
different approach

00:28:44.300 --> 00:28:47.690
to empower the enterprise,
that notion of tribal knowledge

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:51.710
and tribal contribution to get
that governance and culture out

00:28:51.710 --> 00:28:53.640
into the edge and
bring that back in.

00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:55.640
EVREN ERYUREK: And there
will be multiple tools.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:57.800
Don't be really
locked into, I got

00:28:57.800 --> 00:28:59.608
to have one tool
that does everything.

00:28:59.608 --> 00:29:01.400
There will be multiple
services that you're

00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:02.820
going to put together.

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:05.930
Think about the flexibility, try
to solve it around the problem

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:07.550
that you have in hand.

00:29:07.550 --> 00:29:09.820
Pull one thread all
the way out, and see

00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:10.820
what we can actually do.

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:12.020
MICHELE GOETZ: Exactly.

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:17.520
And so measure it based
off of the IT objectives,

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:20.523
the governance objectives,
and the business objectives.

00:29:20.523 --> 00:29:22.940
And that's ensuring that you're
striking the right balance

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:25.910
between what is the value
your data is bringing, as well

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:29.090
as protecting you on
the back end from risk.

00:29:29.090 --> 00:29:32.950
EVREN ERYUREK: And don't think
governance as compliance only.

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:35.480
It's an enabler for
you, what you're

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:39.920
going to be doing with those
capabilities for your business.

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:43.265
A few things we're
doing today on Google.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:48.120
We actually announced just
about all these things,

00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:50.330
so I can talk about it now.

00:29:50.330 --> 00:29:54.410
Some of you may have been
using our Cloud DLP services.

00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:56.370
Know where your data is.

00:29:56.370 --> 00:30:00.050
You heard about our
programs yesterday, Anthos.

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:02.450
And you can actually
use it on your

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:06.560
on prem, hybrid
facilities, or in GCP.

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:08.400
This is your data
loss prevention.

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:11.210
This is a fantastic
tool for those of you

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:14.900
who are concerned about masking
the data that should not

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:16.397
be seen by others.

00:30:16.397 --> 00:30:17.480
You can actually train it.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:20.690
You've been possibly
using it today.

00:30:20.690 --> 00:30:25.190
We announced our metadata
management cloud Data Catalog.

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:27.050
This is a capability
that actually

00:30:27.050 --> 00:30:30.740
helps for you to
discover your assets,

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:33.613
metadata wherever
your data might be.

00:30:33.613 --> 00:30:35.030
And it's a capability
that you can

00:30:35.030 --> 00:30:38.378
integrate with other tools
that are available on-site.

00:30:38.378 --> 00:30:39.920
It might be on-prem
tools that you're

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:42.230
using through the
metadata exchange

00:30:42.230 --> 00:30:44.520
that you can actually
achieve some of these things.

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:46.820
These are all there
for you to discover.

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:49.250
On top of it, you
can build some policy

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:51.530
management systems,
access control

00:30:51.530 --> 00:30:53.240
systems that you can build.

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:56.090
You have an integrated
identity access management

00:30:56.090 --> 00:31:00.950
that I am sure many of you
have been using already today

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:05.060
that helps you identify,
create those policies.

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:08.180
Types of personas, we can
have what type of data

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:11.010
access where and
when and how long.

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:13.340
These are all things
that you can do today.

00:31:13.340 --> 00:31:17.270
We have a very
aggressive roadmap

00:31:17.270 --> 00:31:20.840
to really drive what
we can help you with.

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:22.970
There's a lot of things
that we can provide you

00:31:22.970 --> 00:31:26.370
around the principles of
who can access to what data.

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:28.680
Many of you have
come to us and said,

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:32.270
hey, I'm dealing with this
thing and here are my PII data.

00:31:32.270 --> 00:31:35.450
I don't want anyone in my
organization to have access,

00:31:35.450 --> 00:31:37.760
but I want my analysts
to be able to do it.

00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:41.030
But just mask that data
because it has PII data.

00:31:41.030 --> 00:31:43.230
I don't want to
lose sight of that.

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:46.790
But only those people who have
access can actually control it.

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:48.710
So we are providing
those capabilities.

00:31:48.710 --> 00:31:50.740
And there is of
course table levels.

00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:52.327
There is of course
the role levels.

00:31:52.327 --> 00:31:53.660
There's a lot of things in here.

00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:57.050
There's retention policies
that you want to run,

00:31:57.050 --> 00:31:58.980
or deletion policies
that you want to run.

00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:00.800
Those are all part
of our roadmaps

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:02.450
that we're talking
about in here.

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:05.240
And we will be able
to really provide you

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:09.770
administrative tools
that helps you, gives

00:32:09.770 --> 00:32:13.100
you knobs that you can
actually provide solutions

00:32:13.100 --> 00:32:16.040
within your organizations,
because you need those to be

00:32:16.040 --> 00:32:19.640
able to drive your policies
within your organization,

00:32:19.640 --> 00:32:23.120
around your business
just as it fits for you.

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:28.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

